  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe fair weather cumulus has largely been confined to western WI\nand southeastern MN so far this afternoon with some wispy high\nclouds also moving through on what is otherwise a gorgeous day \nacross the region. Temperatures are comfortable in the low to \nmid 70s expected to reach the mid to upper 70s this afternoon \nbefore dropping back into the 50s tonight. Tomorrow should be a \nbit warmer than today, back to the low to mid 80s as breezy \nsoutherly winds are expected ahead of what will be our next \nchance for precipitation arriving on Friday via a weak cold \nfront. The timing of the front has trended earlier within the \nlast 24 hours of model runs, generally arriving around 12 hours \nearlier than previously forecast by most of the guidance as we \nhave just reached the range where some of the CAMs are starting \nto pick up on it. The earlier timing will allow the strongest \nshowers to arrive from the northwest with a bit of nocturnal low\nlevel jet enhancement, however by the time it reaches the MPX \nCWA it will be after sunrise such that they should remain fairly\nweak. Due to how weak the surface low and frontal boundary are,\nwe are not expecting any sort of significant widespread rains \nor even storms from this round, with most of the area being \nfairly dry or less than a few hundredths of an inch. Those who \nmanage to see a slightly stronger shower or even a weak \nthunderstorm could see a tenth of an inch or two at the most \nthrough the end of Friday.\n\nThere is still a notable warm bias within the GEFS/FV3 suite of \nguidance as we head through the weekend and into early next \nweek, as they are producing a much stronger ridge to the \nsouthwest which is allowing for a much greater degree of lower \nlevel warming compared to the much cooler EPS/ECMWF. The NBM has\ngenerally favored the EPS solution with highs in the low to mid\n80s through the weekend and into early next week, which seems \nmost realistic as the FV3 core likely is keeping a lingering \nwarm bias due to the hot weather we observed throughout July. On\ntop of the warm bias, there are concerns due to the upstream \nflow being sourced from a region with active wildfires resulting\nin upper level smoke which would help knock down temperatures \nby a degree or two. Weaker lower level warming would also give \nus better chances for fair weather cumulus or even some isolated\nstorm activity by reducing any sort of lower level cap and \nallowing us to tap into some instability. Given the upper level \nflow favoring west-northwest to northwesterly, some ridge-rider \nstyle shortwave impulses are also possible, further adding to \nthe daily cloud cover/isolated storm chances. All these things \ncombined, we generally favor the cooler solution compared to the\nred hot GEFS/FV3. As we get a bit closer and into the shorter- \nCAM ranges, we should have a better idea on diurnal thunderstorm\nchances. For now, the best chances remain Friday and Saturday \nnight, with low confidence afterwards.",
